The black box in your car?
While U.S. road planners are grappling with a shortage of funds to improve the highway sympling, many people are looking for a solution in a small black box, which is fitted with cleaning on the dashboard of your car.
This device, which tracks every mile driven by a motor-driver and transmit that information to the authorities, has become an issue of a controversial effort for Washington and the state planning office to renovate the old systems to finance America's major roads.
In general, a boring work like highway planning has also suddenly become an issue of in-depth debates and vibrant alliances.
The miles you've vided, and possibly keep details of the space you've vrived — and then use this information to create support in favor of allowing the government to use these black boxes — have been found with libertarian environmental groups.
The tea party is stunned.
The U.S. Civical Freedoms Association is also very concerned by raising a variety of privacy issues.
While the Congress could not agree whether to take further action or not, many states are not waiting.
They're discovering how in the next decade they can get into a system in which the driver pays for every mile fixed on the road.
Tens of thousands of motorists have already taken a black box for test drives, some of which have GPS monitoring.
It is a truly essential item for our country.
"It's not a matter of being selected or not chosen by us," says Hassan Ikhrata, executive director of the South California Government Union, plans to begin to track every mile fixed by motor-conductors in Callifornia by the year 2025.
There is going to be a change in the way we pay taxes.
technology to do this.
The main reason for this to happen is that the country's highway trust fund has almost gone bankrupt, funded by U.S. citizens by paying taxes on gas pumps.
American people don't buy as much gas as before.
Now the car in a gallon gas lasts more miles than before.
There has been no increase in federal tax in the last 20 years, which is 18.4 cents per gallon.
Due to the high value of gas, politicians refrain from increasing a single penny in taxes.
"Gas tax is absolutely not converactible," says Lee Munich, a transport policy expert at the University of Minnesota.
Their state has recently installed tracking devices in 500 cars to testive the per-mile payment system.
He says, "It will serve as the most reasoned option in the long run," he says.
Wonks call it a mileage-based user fee.
It's not surprising that this idea looks good to urban liberals, as it may, for example, to make changes in the way of driving a car, which can help reduce jams and reduce greenhouse gases.
California's planners are eager for this system while they are developing strategies to meet the targets set in aspirational global warming laws of the state.
But the Chairman of the Home Transport Committee, Rip. Bill Schuster (A-PA) also considers the black box to be the most viable long-term option.
Independent sellers in the Region Foundation are also in favor of payment per mile by the driver.
"It's not just doing what's going into a blind well," said Adrian Mooer, vice-president of the policy in the region.
People are paying more directly for what they are receiving.
The campaign has also been endorsed by two former U.S. transport secretaries, who, in its report in 2011, had graced Congress to move towards per-mile payments.
The U.S. Senate had last year approved a 90-million trial project that would have included 10,000 cars.
But while dealing with the concerns of the rural law-builders who were representing individuals who had to walk too many miles to go to work on a day or going to the city, the leadership of the House abolished that resolution there.
However, many states and cities themselves are moving in this direction.
It has the most interested Auregone, who has listed 5,000 drivers in the country's largest experiment.
These drivers will soon pay mileage charges instead of gas tax to the state.
Nevada has already completed the preliminary test.
New York City is also moving in this direction.
Illinois is trying with trucks on a limited basis.
The I-95 alliance, which includes the 17 State Department of Transportation (including Maryland, Pennsylvania, Virginia and Florida) with the Eastern Seaboard, are studying how they can implement this change.
This idea did not get support everywhere.
In Newada, where this device was installed on the cars of 50 voluntary car drivers shortly ago, the drivers were very impatient thinking that the government could monitor each of their steps.
Alauddin Khan, who guides strategic and performance management in the Nevada Transport Department, says, "Anxiety was a major problem about Big Brother and other things of the same."
It's not something that people wanted.
As the test progressed, Nevada's ACLU warned on its website: "It would be quite easy to convert these devices into a full tracking device."
There is no need to develop a vast, unmanageable technology-structural structure, which has to be carefully disseminated to keep records of every person.
Nevada is one of the many states that are doing everything possible to get affordable technology, with the help of which the state can get a description of how many miles the car is running every day, but not where and at what time it is running.
"""If you can do this, people can feel more comfortable."
The search for this technology led some state agencies to a small California start-up called True Mileage.
This firm was originally not in the business of assisting state tax drivers.
It was trying to enter the emerging market of auto insurance, in which drivers had to pay on the basis of their mileage.
But the device that it is testing was liked by highway planners, because it does not use GPS and provides very limited information, which is uploaded by the modem at a fixed time interval.
"If you don't track people's speed and their location, they'll be more interested in adopting this method," Ryan Morrison, chief executive of True Mileage, said.
Some big mistakes have been made in these initial programs of the state.
To do this, there are very less cost-effective and low-intering methods.
In Oregon, planners are using to give drivers different options.
They can choose the device of GPS or without GPS.
Or choose not to take any devices again, but can choose to pay the prescribed fees based on the average miles to be decided by the residents of the state.
Other places hope that this retention can be sold to worried citizens by providing more convenience rather than less in the device.
In New York City, transport authorities are attempting to develop a taxing device that will also have the facility to pay parking meter fees, "paying according to your drive" will provide insurance, and create real-time speed data from other drivers that motorists will be able to use to avoid traffic.
"Motor-drivers will be attracted to participation due to the benefits derived from it," the city planning document mentions.
However, some transport planners are surprised that perhaps all the discussion on payments according to the miles is big distraction.
The Metropolitan Transport Commission in the San Francisco Gulf region has said that Congress can deal with the problem of the Bankruptal Highway Trust funds very easily by increasing the gas tax.
Drivers of hybrid or other cars can also be charged for an additional one-time or annual fees whose vehicles do not use too much gas so that they pay a reasonable portion.
"Whell surgery is needed when aspirin can work," said Randy Rantscheller, director of legislative and public affairs of the commission.
If we do this, crores of car drivers will be worried about their privacy and many other things.
