In low-lying areas, intermittent rains continued to lash between the eyes of the sun.
With this, the cold wave has started in the entire valley.
The minimum temperature was recorded at 4.8 °C in Srinagar, minus 0.2 degrees Celsius in Gulmarg and -1.8 degrees Celsius in Leh.
LEH was the coldest in the entire state.
At the same time, due to rains and snowfall, the strategically important Srinagar-Leh National Highway has been closed for traffic.
There has been heavy snowfall at the Zojila Pass.
This is the fourty-first and first heavy snowfall in the hilly areas of Kashmir.
Due to Western Disturbance Activism, snowfall and low-lying areas of the valley continued till late in the evening.
Normal life has also been affected due to the cold.
People rushed to their homes to avoid the cold wave.
Meanwhile, power supply was also disrupted in many areas.
Srinagar recorded a maximum temperature of 13.7 degrees Celsius, four notches below normal.
Meanwhile, heavy rains lashed Jammu in the wee hours of Thursday, leading to heavy rains.
Jammu recorded a minimum temperature of 16.6 degrees Celsius and a maximum of 27.0 degrees Celsius.
According to the India Meteorological Department (IMD), the impact of the western disturbance has started decreasing and the weather will improve from Friday.
According to Sonam Lots, director of Srinagar-based Meteorological Department, the weather is likely to remain dry in the valley from November 1st to 7th.
