Officials investigating the matter have clarified that in case of non-payment, recovery action will be taken by sealing accounts.
The demand for sweets increases in view of Diwali.
In view of this, the sale of substandard and counterfeit mawas increases.
Chemicals are used to make sweets more shiny, colorful and sweet, which is dangerous for health.
The Food and Drug Administration Department has launched a campaign against fake mawa and chemical sweets.
The department seiscovered Rs 70-thousan Mawa from Madhya Pradesh and Rs 5 lakh from Quetta colony.
With the increase in the demand for sweets in the market, the demand for substandard mawa from Madhya Pradesh has also increased.
The department received information about bringing substandard mave kept in dirt from Madhya Pradesh to Nagpur.
A consignment of Mawe is being ordered from Transport Garage in Nagpur.
The department took possession of this mawa, the price of which was said to be 70 thousand rupees.
The fear of action did not anyone.
The department destroyed it by considering unclame (undisclosed) property.
The department raided Jeetendra Caterrers in Quetta Colony and recovered a glass of about Rs 5 lakh.
Sodium hydrosulfite was used to shine and last for a long time.
The sample will be sent to the laboratory.
S.S. Deshmukh, co-commissioner of the department, said the campaign would continue to be a campaign to nab chematic sweets and substandard mawa.
In the next few days, information has been given by the department to intensify this campaign.
Consuming these sweets can also lead to toxins.
The department asked the sweet traders to make good milk, sweets from mawa.
The department has appealed to the people to be careful of fake sweets and mawas.
