CDC Issues Allergy Guidelines for Children for Schools
On Wednesday, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention issued guidelines to control food allergies of children in the school.
For the first time, such guidelines have been issued by the U.S. government, as the number of children going to school suffering from food allergies is increasing.
Now one out of every 20 children in the U.S. have food allergies.
CDC found that between 1997 and 2007, there has been an increase of 18 per cent in food allergy cases in children.
In this direction, it has been communicated for the schools how teachers and staff should be informed about the food allergies of the children, and how to take care of it in case of an allergic reaction.
Schools have also been suggested to keep the stock of apinephrine to cope with potentially life-threatening anaphyllaxis — the auto-injector of the epipen brand that is most commonly used.
The state assembly has recently updated the rules, allowing schools to easily keep a stock of apnephrine in large quantities.
This report also includes a list of common symptoms transmitted by children suffering from an allergic reaction.
Children can say, "It's like it's something pricking in my t tongue," "It looks like there's hair on my tongue", or "I'm getting tingling in my tongue."
