The dream of firecracker traders to do business in the firecracker market in the transport area did not come true.
Firecracker traders have been trapped in Majhadar after the High Court order.
Neither they can now do business in the temporary firecracker market built at Ranjit Avenue, nor have they been issued licenses for business in the transport area.
During his visit, the commission constituted by the Punjab and Haryana High Court declared the temporary firecracker market at Ranjit Avenue unsafe.
Due to lack of proper arrangements there, a fine of ten lakh rupees was also imposed on the Deputy Commissioner of Amritsar.
In its order, the High Court has reprimanded the administrative officials of Amritsar, saying that the administration has not been able to solve any difficulty of firecracker traders for nine years.
They could not be provided proper space in the city.
After this, proper security has been made and firecracker traders were asked to issue a license for business in the market in Shipgarh itself.
After this order, the firecrackers may be happy, but the police turned away on their happiness on Friday.
A team from the police's Aslaha license branch reached the firecracker market and sold the form by putting a counter there.
It was impossible to fulfill them in one day the conditions that were stated to him thereafter.
ACP East Gaurav Garg, In-charge of Aslaha License Branch Anup Kumar, SHO Sultanwind Arun Sharma reached the firecracker market.
He expressed discontensation over the arrangements made by the firecracker traders.
Therefore, licenses were not issued to firecracker traders.
There is a day left for Diwali.
Due to this, the firecraggers are worried.
They are losing crores of rupees.
Avinash Singh, Rakesh Seth, Harish Dhawan, Harish Dua, Desraj, Satnam Singh, Manjinder Singh and Ginni said they would stake claim to the district administration if they lost crores of rupees.
Angry firecracker traders took out a protest march from Shipgarh to Ranjit Avenue on Friday night.
They shouted slogans against the police and the district administration.
After this, the business was also closed in the temporary firecracker market at Ranjit Avenue.
