The new anti-nicotine vaccine can eliminate the enjoyment of smoking
Scientists have developed an anti-nicotine vaccine that can eliminate the fun from smoking.
The same dose of the vaccine was able to protect mice from nicotine addiction for the rest of their lives.
There is a need for further testing before the test is started on humans, which may take many years, but Ronald Crystal, a professor at Weale Cornell Medical College in New York, said the initial signs are positive.
"We have a great hope that this type of vaccine strategy can eventually help millions of smokers who have attempted to quit smoking using all the methodologies in the market today, but they found that their addiction to nicotine is much more powerful than these existing methodologies."
The new vaccine has a harmless virus designed to deliver genetic information aimed at creating anti-nicotine antibodies.
The virus selectively incrucates the liver cell, which then begins to form a permanent stream of antibodies.
Entibodies discover any nicotine molecules in the blood, and prevents the nicotine reproaks inside the smoker by neutralizing them before reaching the brain.
In the test, the vaccinated rats, which were given nicotine later, continued to carry out their normal activities.
Researchers say that but the rat who was not given the vaccine "good", it was a sign that nicotine had reached his brain.
This experiment was described in the journal Science Translational Medicine.
The earlier tobacco vaccines had failed because they had antibodies.
It had to be given at regular interval so that the level of antibodies was maintained, but it proved to be expensive and impractical.
But the price of the new vaccine is expected to be very high, as the liver cell will become an antibody's factory.
Professor Krystle said that if the human vaccine of the future is completely safe, it can be given to children before the temptation to try cigarettes, so that they can be protected from nicotine addiction.
But it will probably be used by smokers to quit smoking.
"They'll find out if they start smoking again they won't get any pleasure from smoking due to a nicotine vaccine, which can aid to get rid of their habits," he said.
British scientists described the result as interesting, but warned of the need for a lot of research to be done.
