FAA: Aircraft passengers can now use gadgets in the aircraft (but can't make calls from a cell phone)
Aircraft passengers will be able to use their electronic devices to read, work, play games, watch movies, listen to music - but will not be able to talk to their cellphones — is under high-pregnated new guidelines issued by the Federal Aviation Administration on Thursday.
However, commuters should not expect these changes immediately.
Speaking at a news conference, FAA Administrator Michael Huerta said how fast these changes are implemented will be different in each airline.
The airlines have to show the FAA how their planes complete the new guidelines, and how their crew members are updating the training booklets and rules for collecting devices to reflect the guidelines.
The FAA has stated that it has already received plans from some airlines to expand the use of portable electronic devices on the aircraft.
Delta and JetBlue are among the airlines that have already submitted plans.
"Delibated on the planning situation, we can very quickly approve the extended use of the electronic device," the FAA said in a statement.
At present, passengers are required to turn off their smartphones, tablets, and other devices when the airport gates are closed.
They are expected not to turn on the aircraft until it arrives at an altitude of 10,000 feet and is allowed by the captain.
Passengers are expected to turn off their devices again when the plane lands, and don't turn it on until it's completely unloaded.
"""Under the new guidelines, airlines whose aircraft are completely safe from electronic interruptions may allow their passengers to use the device at the time of flying, landing the aircraft and at the time of taxiing,"" the FAA said."
Most new airline companies and other aircraft which have been modified in such a way that passengers can use WiFi at higher altitudes are expected to meet these criteria.
Laura Gleading, President of the Association of Professional Flight Attendants, has welcomed these changes.
"When the policy is safely implemented — and we will work closely with airlines companies to do that — it will be a win-win for both sides," Glading said in a statement.
When it comes to this issue, we are bored feeling like "hall monitors."
"But surfing, email exchanges, sending or receiving messages, or downloading the internet at a height of less than 10,000 feet will still be restricted,"" the agency said."
Passengers will be asked to keep their smartphones, tablets and other devices in airplane mode.
So there will still be no conversations with friends, an online scrabble-like game, the well-known event associated with this is that in the year 2011, when actor Alec Baldwin was playing on his smartphone while the aircraft was parked at the door, and when he refused to turn off the device.
And heavy equipment such as laptops etc. will still have to be stored because of their bouncing that the other person in the cabin will be worried about hurting.
Cellphone calls will also continue to be banned during the flight.
The Federal Communications Commission does not have the regulatory authority related to phone calls.
The FAA may remove restrictions on some electronic devices during the flight and landing.
Last month, Mark Rosencan of the National Transportation Safety Board, who is a national transport safety expert at CBS News, said the cell phone is still considered a risk.
"Celle phones are a really serious issue, not just because they can potentially cause disruptions to navigation-related devices, but also because they can disrupt cell phone towers when they're in the air," Rojenker said.
The Industry Advisory Committee, set up by the FAA to examine the issue, has recommended last month that the government should allow greater utilisation of individual electronic devices.
In recent years, the pressure on the FAA to reduce the restrictions from their use has been increasing.
Critics like Claire McCaskilluck, De-Mo have said that there is no legitimate security reason for the prohibition.
Improving these restrictions is becoming even more difficult because the use of the device is omnipresent.
Some studies also indicate that about a third of passengers forget or ignore the instructions to turn off their devices.
FAA started banning the use of electronic devices by passengers in the year 1996 when reports of disruption of navigation and communication devices were received due to passengers carrying FM radio and high-tech gadgets at that time.
New airline companies are more dependent on electrical systems than the previous generation of planes, but they have also been designed and approved by the FAA to have resistance to electronic interference.
Airlines have been proposing Wi-Fi use at cruising heights from passengers for many years.
Planes modified for Wi-Fi systems are also more resistant to disruption.
Huerta said that most airline companies should meet the eligibility criteria for greater utilization of electronic devices under the new guidelines.
Today's electronic devices emit very little radio transmission compared to previous generation of electronic devices.
For example, e-readers is an absolutely nominal transmission when the page is turned.
However, transmissions are strong while downloading or sending data by the device.
Amazon.com has also been among those who favor reducing the restrictions on the use of the device by passengers.
In 2011, the company's executives loaded their entire Kindle e-readers in airlines and blew it up to test the problems, but there was no such a problem.
The FAA Advisory Committee has expressed mixed feelings about the risk caused by the use of the device.
Douglas Kid of the National Aircraft Passenger Association said he believes that the disruption caused by the device is real, even if the risk is nominal.
Other members of the committee said that there are reports described only by the pilot in support of being disrupted by the device in the aircraft system, most of which are very old.
The committee members, however, recommended that the FAA pilot be permitted to order the passengers to switch off the device at the time of unloading the aircraft with low visibility.
The Travel Industry Group has welcomed the changes and has called them a common knowledgeable facility for people travelling in the latest era of technology.
“We are delighted that the FAA has recognized that a traveler travelling with pleasure is not inconsistent with rescue and safety,” said Roger Doe, CEO of the U.S. Travel Association.
