Is the upper class of Europe ready to do business with Britain?
Business for Britain was launched in April and a pledge was made to bring the business together and determine what changes our relationship with U.K.'s employment and prosperity maker E.
For that, we conducted the largest and most detailed survey of UK business leaders and asked them what they had about Britain's business with the EU.
U-Gov took the views of 1000 business leaders, who are representatives of Britain's commercial size, regions and locations.
The conclusions of this survey can be surprising for a lot of people.
We found that a large part of businessmen now wants to be exported out of Europe, and focusing on countries that are becoming modern and growing, while EU member countries are becoming sedentary.
He wants the government to prioritize new trade relations with countries like China, India and Brazil, and not embroished in the long-term and difficult process of reformation of mysterious institutions of the EU.
When asked about their views on specific policy areas ranging from monopoly regulation to product law, most business leaders believed that the control of these conclusive qualifications should come back to Westminster.
Common dissatisfaction about a single market could be seen very easily, with businessmen saying that the price of Brussel's regulation is much higher than the benefits of becoming part of Europe's trading sector - even 40 percent of giant businessmen who favored traditionally European companies agreed.
In the end and most clearly, our survey of business leaders found that, a large part clearly wants the UK to move on the path of trade-based relationship and treaty change rather than politics with the EU.
This conclusion, seen in the levels of major business groups, showed that the businessmen are motivating towards a "meaningful change" that would give the U.K. its lost power back.
It's a very lighty price — the treaty change for the UK and getting a better deal changed by 16 per per cent in favor of sticking to the EU.
The Prime Minister should not be in any doubt: This survey shows that British practitioners support his plan to negotiate on the terms of Britain's membership in the EU.
It also demonstrated that the practitioners expect the current balance of power to make a meaningful change in favour of the U.K.
A better deal is possible for British businesses, and it is becoming imperative very fast, as the eurozone is moving towards the ecosystem and the fiscal union.
Priority must be jobs and growth in the UK, as the findings of our survey show that for practitioners it means a new focus on trade and a fundamental change in the Brussel's regulation methodology.
