The scientists have further highlighted what is the connection between the movements of the dog's tail and its mood.
Previous researches have shown that happy dogs move their ringles more to the right (from the point of view of a dog), while restless dogs move their tails mainly to the left.
But now scientists say that fellow dogs can recognize this subtle distinction of tail and express their feedback on it.
This study has been published in the journal Currant Biology.
Georgia Vellartigara, a neuroscientist professor from the University of Trento, explained: "In humans all know very well that the left and right parts of the brain are involved in the stimulation that generates positive or negative emotions."
Here we have tried to see it in other species as well.
He pointed out that the right part of the brain in dogs, like humans, was accounted of for left-hand movements and the opposite order, and the two hemispheres played different roles in emotions.
To learn more about how dogs react to one-sided riddle shaking by other dogs, the researchers kept an eye on animals watching films from other dogs.
He measured the heartbeats of pet dogs and analyzed their behavior.
It probably doesn't take much time for us to understand why their squirrels sometimes move on one side, sometimes on the other.
Prof. Vellartigara pointed out: "We introduced the dogs with dog films — in the shadow-picture to deal with the realistic version or entangling other issues, and we could look at the tail movement and presented the tail more left or right."
When the animals saw an emotional dog shaking their tell on the right (from the point of view of a dog), they were completely relaxed.
But when he saw the tail changing direction, moving mainly to the left (from the point of view of the dog), his heartbeat increased and he looked restless.
According to Prof. Vellartigara, he does not think that the dogs were deliberately communicating with each other through these antics.
Instead, they think dogs have learned from their own experience about what act they should be worried about and what not.
He said: "If you've met other dogs many times, and it's related to more friendly behavior if they constantly move their tails aside, and the right part presents less friendly behavior, you react based on the same experience."
Researchers say these findings can provide better information to owners, veterinarians and trainers about animal sentiments.
John Bradshaw, an expert of the University of Bristol's School of Veteternary Science, said that this is not the first study to investigate whether the left or right part is important for dogs.
Last year's team from the University of Lincoln found thave their head to the left when they see an aggressive dog and turn their heads to the right when they look at a happy dog.
In other research from the University of Victoria in Canada, he pointed out: "In contrast to the Italian study — when the robot dog's tail was shaked to the less on the left that was more likely to get closer rather than being restless."
He said that this difference was because in various studies dogs were not considering animals or robot dogs as a fully squean.
He made it clear that the study of how dogs react to real dogs can help.
"While there has been considerable evidence from different mammals that two parts of the brain are used for different purposes, more detailed information is yet to be obtained - and in this case dogs are no exception," he said.
However, given the ease with which their behavior can be recorded, it seems that it will probably not take much time for us to understand why their tail sometimes moves on one side, sometimes on the other.
