RBS suspended two forex traders
The Royal Bank of Scotland has suspended two traders from its forex exchange department, receiving this information from two people, the other indication is moving at a rapid pace by the global investigations conducted by regulators in suspicious manipulation of the currency market.
Some of the world's largest banks, including UBS Barclage, Deutsche Bank and RBS, have confirmed that they are collaborating with regulators in an investigation into the world's largest financial market that exchanges $5.3 trillion each day.
These two traders will be the first employees of the RBS, who have been suspended as the case appears to be a repetition of the Libor interbank loan manipulation scandal.
The bank has refused to comment about the suspension, confirming that the bank has received a request for information from the regulators this month.
Two weeks ago, the bank had said, "We are investigating into this matter, and we are fully cooperating with the FCA and other regulators."
Last month, experts from the matter had said that RBS had sent to the former Trader and the records of emails and prompt messages received from them to the UK Regulatory, Financial Ethics Authority.
This trader named Richard Euscher left RBS in the year 2010, and is believed to have been discharged from its current position in J.P. Morguan, the European Head of Forex Spot Trading.
Rohan Ramchandani, the head of European spot trading in City, went on leave last week, while former senior currency traders in Barclage and UBS were suspended by Standard Chartered this week.
None of these traders have been accused of any wrongdoing.
Experts from the case said that Mr. Eusher's Accelerated Message Group includes bankers of Barclage and CITIGRoop.
The UBS said it has taken action against some of its employees this week after the Swiss regulator, Finma, reported that it was investigating the suspicious manipulation of the forex market in many Swiss banks.
At least six global authoritative authorities — the European Commission, Finma, Switzerland's Competition Authority of Weico, FCA, the Department of Justice at the U.S., and the Hong Kong Monetary Authority — are investigating accusations that bankers were misbehaving for their favourable fluctuations in the currency market.
HSBC, Citigroup, J.P. Morgan, and Credit Suisse have also carried out an internal investigation, or obtained a request for information on behalf of the regulators.
Banks are closely scrutinising instant messages and emails for several years in search of clues of any wrong activities.
The news about the investigation has created panic among traders in a sector who have been one of the most profitable individuals in the trading units of investment banks for the past several years, but they are facing the challenge this year, as opportunities for speculators have decreased due to low fluctuations in currencies.
Some bankers tried to suppress the matter by saying that it is impossible to manipulate the very vast and highly liquid forex exchange market, but senior traders say it does not necessarily come true.
Senior Trader stated that the fragmentation of liquidity between different trading platforms despite a huge magnitude of forex buying and sale and increasing use of their own platforms by banks means "you can start impacting the market at a slightly lower ticket price."
The news came on the same day Credit Suisse announced the retirement from its Exchange Traded Funds desk in London to the trader's dismissal this week, which lost nearly $6 million at the end of the previous year.
The Bank immediately notified the relevant authorities and is cooperating with its regulators.
"We are confident that the trader had acted alone, and has been controlled," Credit Suisse said.
