An economic lawsuit has been filed against Google, Sasmang, Huawei in the case of Nortail patent.
The group, owned by thousands of former Nortel patents, on Thursday has filed many patent lawsuits against Google, including cell phone makers, which it had overtaken the Nortel bankruptcy auction.
An association named Rockstar, which bought local patents for 4.5 billion, has sued Samsung Electronics Co Ltd, HTC Corporation, Huawei and four other companies for a patent violation at the U.S. District Court in Texas.
Rockstar is jointly owned by Apple Microsoft, Blackberry, Ericssion and Sony.
Google is accused of violating seven patents.
These patents also include the technology that combines the words to be searched on the Internet from relevant advertising, which is the main part of Google's search business.
Representatives of Samsung, Huawei, HTC and Rockstar could not be contacted immediately.
Samsung, Huawei, and HTC make all these phones that run on Google's Android operating system, which is strongly opposed to Apple and Microsoft mobile products.
In 2011, Google made an initial bid of $900 million for Nortel patents.
Google finally proposed $4.4 billion, increasing the bid several times.
After losing to Rockstar at Nortel patents, Google bought Motorola Mobility for $12.5 billion, one of the main reasons why Motorola had a long list of patents.
"Gabog has violated, and has been consistently inviolable despite losing in an attempt to acquire patents-in-sute in the auction," the indictment said.
Rockstar has called for increased compensation against Google, as it is claimed to have claimed that Google's violation of patents is mischievous.
Confidence in the first elected president independently in Egypt
Mohamed Morsi has sworn in his office, but his Victory Diwas could hardly put an end to the political struggle in Egypt.
Islamist Mohammed Morsi pledged "New Egypt" while taking the oath of office as the country's first independently elected president, who was sacked 16 months ago.
On his formal establishment before the Supreme Constitutional Court, Morsi has become the first Islamist president of the world's independently elected Islamist, and Egypt's fifth heads of state after the monarchy was ousted nearly 60 years ago.
He took an oath on the banks of the Nile River in front of 18 judges of the black cloked court on his chair, which looked like an ancient Egyptian temple.
"We are aspiring for a better future, a new Egypt and another republic," Morsi said during a formal ceremony shown in a living broadcast on national television.
"Today the people of Egypt have laid the foundation for a new life — complete freedom, true democracy and stability," the 60-year-old Morsi, an engineer trained at the party's U.S., said that "today the people of Egypt have laid the foundation for a new life — complete freedom, true democracy and stability."
Hundreds of soldiers and policemen guarded the building and came to Morsi in small convoys of cars shortly after 11 a.m. local time.
Only a few hundred supporters gathered outside the court to congratulate the new president from the time of Mubarak, with traffic being stopped for a while, so that his motor convoy would normally come out of the busy road, which connects the center of the city to its southern suburbs.
Morsi's personal prestignation, ridiculed as a non-charisman "eligible tyre" of the Brotherhood, rose after his victory, and his speech on Friday presented him not only as Islamists, but as a candidate for everyone who wants to complete the work of the 2011 revolution against Dictatory Mubarak.
Dressed wearing a blue business suit and a red tie, Morsi took an oath of office in front of judges sitting in a wooden panel chamber, saying "Today Egypt is a citizen, national, constitutional and modern nation."
Morsi then went to the University of Keiro, where he had to deliver his inaugural address.
He was officially welcomed by the military band by playing the national tune, while they were standing in a careful posture.
Military ruler Field Marshain Hussein Tantavi was also present.
On his arrival, hundreds of people present in the lecture room at the main university there welcomed with the slon that "the army and the people are one hand."
Established in 1908 as a stronghold of secular education, the University of Cairo later became a stronghold of Islamist student groups in the year 1970.
On Friday, Morsi took a symbolic oath at Tahrir Square, the creading of the revolution that ended Mubarak's dictatorial regime last year, and vowed to withdraw the President's powers taken away from his office by the military council.
But by agreeing to take an official oath before the Court instead of the competent oath of Parliament in the customarily, they bowed to the will of the army, indicating that the struggle for power will continue.
Morsi's speech in Tahrir Scwire was filled with dramatic popular currencies.
