Secretary of State John Kerry unexpectedly acknowledged that in some cases the U.S. espionage has surpassed.
At the same time, he claimed that innocent people have not been tortured under the Intelligence Surveillance Programme.
Kerry's confession came while addressing the Annual Summit of the Open Government Partnership held in London through a video link.
"""I assure you that innocent people are not being tortured in this process."
But in an attempt to get the information, inappropriate methods have been adopted in some cases.
Meanwhile, an important committee of the U.S. Congress has approved a bill limiting the surveillance activities of the National Security Agency (NSA).
It aims to make surveillance more transparent and reinstate the confidence among the people who have been injured due to a large-scale phone tapping revelation.
The Senate's move has been welcomed by six US companies including Google, Yahoo.
NSA has also breached the data centers of these companies.
The Senate Select Committee on Friday passed the Foreign Intelligence Surveillance Reserving Reforms Bill (FISA), which shred against NSA, by four votes against 11.
The bill mentions a number of restrictions, including prohibiting one-to-rise phone tappers under the U.S. Patriott Act.
Under the FISA, the use of unauthorised data will be deemed to be an offence and a provision has been made to serve ten years in prison.
The bill will become an elaw after the Congress is passed in the House of Representatives and the Senate.
"Senator Diane Festin, chairman of the committee, said: ""The NSA's phone recording programme is under law and I believe it has contributed significantly to the country's security."
However, more transparency should be brought about.
Not only this, the security of the privacy of the public should also be taken care of.
Meanwhile, Google, Yahoo, Microsoft, Apple, AOL and Facebook have endorsed the bill after the NSA's internet surveillance program was revealed.
In a letter to senators, these companies have said that we welcome this proposal of lawmakers who have curbed NSA's large-scale phone records.
Significantly, documents issued by former CIA employee Edward Snowden removed the veil from the actions of the NSA.
There has been a lot of resentment among the international community after the NSA revealed the telephonic telespons monitored by the heads of states of 35 countries, including Germany and France.
