So you used to have travel agents in the last century, say thank you to the Internet?
The flight center is taking this trend forward.
The company has increased its full-year profit forecast, and is expecting a record profit from holidaymakers in Australia and the United Kingdom.
This travel company expects it to have an in-componential profit from $325 million to $340 million in the pre-ti-year tax, compared to $305 million to $315 million in the previous forecast.
If the target is achieved as per current guidance, it will increase by 12 to 17 per cent in a record $290.4 million profit achieved in 2011/12.
Managing Director Graham Turner said that the Flight Center has made a profit of 8 per cent in the first har half, and has made a good start in its second half, especially in Australia and the U.K. non-professional travel.
"So far, 10 of our countries are in profit, and many of our countries are on the path of full-year income before interest and tax contributions," he said.
This includes Australia and the United Kingdom, which are usually our most profitable centers.
The holiday business has gained momentum in the second half in Australia, fulfilling the lack of a slightly weaker domestic corporate travel market.
Similarly, in the U.K., the flight center's leisure business did well, while corporate clients were spending less.
The U.S. business had compensated its ghats in its first harli half, which is expected to give a third consecutive full-year profit.
Shares of the Flight Center were up 3 cents at $38.20 yesterday.
