The schools pledged to pay more attention to mathematics, spelling and grammar.
In English literature courses, students must at least read Shakespeare's drama, a 19th-century novel, romantic poetry, and a synchronous British story after 1914.
To encourage more and more reading, the exam will also include "not seen lessons".
A combined English literature and language course will be abolished.
From the year 2015, students will have to take a separate GCSE in the language, and will have a lot of induction to choose English literature as a separate qualification.
The Department of Education will release a new curriculum for English and Mathematics tomorrow - both of them will be the first subjects in which there will be a revolutionary change.
It will also make changes to other key topics next year.
In a separate move, the examination regulators will display the changes taking place in the structure of GCSE along with off-specific, new grading systems, and fewer courses.
"There was a lot of consensus that we need to improve our testing system to reinvent the confidence of the people," Education Secretary Michael Gov said, adding that the GCSE would be "more challenging, more ambitious and more rigorous."
Studies found that English schools devote less time in mathematics — 116 hours a year or - 3 hours less a week during a session than in most countries.
By comparison, Australian schools provide students with an average of 143 hours a year, while Singapore has an average of 138 hours.
While there is no formal need to give more time to mathematics, coalition sources said the broader mathematics GCSE — the league table was likely to be encouraged to provide additional learning in schools by combining overweight with subjects.
This course will focus more on "real-world problems, including financial mathematics."
