It is going to be expensive to kill mosquitoes to avoid diseases like dengue, malaria, filariasis.
The state government is going to triple the tax on repelants, mattes, liquids used in killing mosquitoes.
The proposal to increase taxes will be placed before the cabinet soon.
The government, which is facing a financial crisis, is looking for all possible avenues to increase earnings these days.
Urban Development Minister Mohammad Azam Khan has been constituted under the chairmanship of Urban Development Minister Mohammad Azam Khan.
According to sources, in the recent resource committee meeting, out of many of the proposals for tax-escalation proposals were rejected by the committee, whose implementation would have had a direct impact on the general public.
However, the committee has given its nod to the proposal to increase the tax on mosquito repelants, coils, mats and liquid.
It is proposed to triple the tax on them.
At present, there are four per cent VAT and one per cent additional taxes.
A total of 12.50 per cent VAT and 1.50 per cent additional taxes are proposed.
The government is estimated to get an additional revenue of around Rs 14 crore.
In order to increase the tax revenue, the Commerce Tax Department has been in favour of increasing the tax on Tendu leaves from five per cent to 14 per cent, but the Financial Resources Committee has rejected the proposal.
The committee believes that tendu leaves are used to make the beedi that the lower strants drink.
Similarly, the committee has not yet given its ability to increase the tax on electronic goods such as transmission wires, saying it could have an impact on the general public due to costlier electricity.
The committee has also not accepted the proposal to impose green taxes on the renewal of commercial vehicles.
