To provide free medical service, progress through soil, rivers and forests
Dr.Jourges Well is delivering free healthcare to the rural areas of Cameroon
Well and his team see hundreds of patients on almost every weekend
There is not too many therapists in West African countries; there is only one physician for 5000 people
Vote here or through your mobile
Dr. Georges Well is one of CNN's top 10 heroes in the year 2013.
You can vote for them or to someone in the other top 10 to make C.N. year's hero.
The winning person will receive $250,000 to continue his extraordinary work.
For 21 years, George's Well had seen his ailing father regained consciousness and fainting, going to the hospital, who was not properly facilitated to help him.
Jamel Well was injured in a car accident near Jounde, the capital of Cameroon, in 1981.
Initially his only hand was fractured, but an increased infection that spread to his brain, and he created an arrogance, which affected the rest of his life.
“There was no neurosurgeon in the cameraoon,” says Georges Well.
If we had the money, we would have taken our father out of Cameroon.
Instead, Well continued to visit highly crowded clinics and hospitals with his father and received whatever treatment he could get.
"It's not easy," Well said.
You leave the house at 5 a.m. hoping that you will be the first person to reach the hospital, but it doesn't happen.
There are a lot of patients there.
Some people can get into the face of death while waiting.
There has not been a lot of change in the situation since the death of Well's father in 202.
According to the World Health Organization, Cameroon has only one physician for every 5000 person.
If compared, there is a therapist for 413 individuals in the United States.
And even if there are physicians there, many residents of Cameroon cannot afford it.
Two out of five people in the country live below the poverty line, and about two-thirds of healthcare expenditure in the country is in the private sector.
"His only problem is poverty," says Well.
And being poor, they cannot enjoy their lives.
Seeing the pain of his father and his countrymen, Well took a firm resolve to to solve it.
Dr. Georges and his team of volunteers have carried out 700 free surgery last year.
He himself became a doctor, and started working as a vascular surgeon at the Center's Hospital in Yonde.
And he launched the non-profit organization ESCOYAM, which goes to rural areas on the weekend to provide free medical services.
Since 2008, he and his group of volunteers have assisted about 32,000 people.
Almost every Friday they and up to about 30 people sit in the vans, tying medical supplies to the terrace and go to rural areas where people need medical treatment.
Sometimes luck doesn't even happen to them.
They have to push the vehicle more than once from rivers and soil.
But when they arrive, they really get the honor of heroes: feasting, singing and dancing, and the best accommodation that can be offered by the community.
Free medical service in these villages is actually the reason to celebrate, and Well - with its great smile and endless energy — involves happily.
The next morning his team begins to meet hundreds of patients.
"We're meeting 500 people on each yatra," Well said.
They come from about 60 km away from the village, and they come on foot.
All these weekend clinics provide a variety of medical services.
Malaria, tuberculosis, malnutrition, diabetes, parasites and sexually transmitted diseases are treated by many.
Other people can get crutches, donated spectacles or a free birth certificate -- this document is required in the school, but many poor families cannot afford it.
In the evening, the team performs simple surical surgery with spatial anesthesia.
Operations are usually carried out at school buildings, town halls or at home; after the procedure, the patient walks and goes to the recovery place, and makes room for the next person.
The group's generator is published in the operating room and sanitizing equipment, and Well and his volunteers wake up early in the morning on Sunday.
It is a wreak pace, but village musicians generally assist in keeping the party motivated.
"They keep playing drums all the night to keep us awake and continue their work," Well said.
On Sunday, their team returns to the city, they are tired, but they are proud of their work.
This group — — Cameroon’s physicians and foreign medical students—have carried 700 free surgeries last year, and they know that their help can change the world of people they help.
One person said that due to free hernia surgery, he will be able to go back to work.
The man said, "This will change the future of me and my family."
In addition to running weekend clinics and working as a surgery doctor in the hospital, Well also works at a private medical clinic around Yonde at night.
He pointed out that this is his second job that finances 60% of his non-profit work; the rest part is met by private donations.
"I don't know when they sleep," said Katy O'Maley, a second-year medical student at the University of Drexel in Philadelphia and a self-sevitator in a well group.
They are always either in the hospital, or attempting to earn money for their organization, so that they keep running these campaigns.
It's an experimental opportunity for medical and nursing students like O'Mally who come from the United States and Europe to participate in Well's expedition, which they can never get in their own country.
"We've been able to gain experimental experience in which we help Dr. Well to hold tools or clear blood stains," said O'Maley.
You will never get the opportunity to do this as a second-year medical student in the United States.
Volunteer students typically spend from their closets to Cameroon arrive, and they often come up with medical supplies for donations.
But after reaching the yunde, their movement, and the expenses of the instructions are borne by the well.
"No doubt they're heroes," says O'Maley.
They give their lives for this organization, and their desire to help the people of Cameroon is never going to end.
For the well, the burden of almost the same work, is not difficulty.
Assisting other people to live a happy life, fulfilling the promise made to their father, these are the things that give them a lot of pleasure.
Well says, "I'm very happy while doing this work."
And I think about my father.
I'm hoping they'll see what I'm doing.
I'm doing this to make people laugh, reduce their pain.
Check the Escovime's website and find out how to help.
