Obama's healthcare proposal was revoked.
"You can continue if you like your health plan," President Obama yesterday withdrew his often-reiterating misinformation promise that "you can continue if you like your health plan."
With millions receiving discancellation information from their providers, Republicans have criticized the president for misleading U.S. citizens in recent times.
Yesterday, Obama changed his real message.
"A lot of people who have health insurance and are useful can continue it," he said in a speech in Boston.
Speaking about the abolitation, what he calls the "guise of news," Obama has graced American citizens receiving these information to buy new coverage in the market.
Most people will be able to get a better deal, a comprehensive healthcare span from the same amount of money or even cheaper.
"You will get a better deal," he said.
The administration has said that it should not come as a surprise that 5 per cent of the population, who buy insurance with their own money, may be forced to change their plans as their coverage is not in line with the standards required under the Affordable Care Act.
