In an unintended confession, John Kerry said that U.S. spying "inappropriately has gone too far."
John Kerry has unintegrately indicated softening U.S's protective attitude on his surveillance program by admitting that sometimes its espionage "inappropriately goes too far."
The Minister of State has also admitted that he is also guilty along with Barack Obama in the matter of being "automatic pilot" towards informant Edward Snowden as a revoltative disclosure about NSA's espionage activities coming to the fore.
This revelation has brought the U.S. government to a diplomatic turmoil in relations with its allies.
"There is no question that the President, me, and others in the government were really aware of some of the things they might have with an automatic pilot, because looking back, the World War II, and the Cold War's difficult years, and then, of course it had the potential in 9/11."
He has become the first high-ranking member of the U.S. government who has admitted that U.S. espionage has crossed its limits, but stressed that no one's rights have been misused.
He said: "In some cases, it has gone too far inappropriately."
And the President is determined to try to make it clear to the people and is still conducting a thorough review so that no person suffers a sense of abuse.
I assure you that nothing is going wrong with innocent people in this process.
Shri Kerry emphasised that, however, N.S.A. was striving for a good purpose, and its surveillance work has saved many lives.
"We are facing a world where people are ready to blow themselves up."
There is radical extremism in the world, which is committed to bleawing people and attacking governments.
So what's the problem if you're able to prevent it before it occurs?
We've actually saved the plane from falling, building collapses, and killing people because we've already been able to know about others' plans.
Meanwhile, the chairman of the Senate Subcommitee for European Affairs on Thursday said the U.S. lawbuilders will go to Europe to assist in addressing concerns abroad about so-called U.S. espionage, and try to convince European countries about the need to continue anti-corruption efforts with the U.S.
Connext Senator Chris Murphy said he spoke to members of the European Parliament and others this week and was worried about the threats of his involvement in counter-terrorism organizations due to the frustration over surveillance by the National Security Agency.
"It's really important for the U.S. national security interests that European countries are with us in the context of our anti-terrorist efforts," Murphy, president of the Senate Foreign Relations Subcommittee of the Democratic Party of the Democratic Party said in an interview.
In order for European countries to clarify that we need to continue to work together in order to fight terrorism, which should not be influenced by their anger about the NSA program.
Reports of the NSA's recording of millions of phone conversations in Europe have brought bitterness in relations with some U.S. affiliates, although the head of the agency said that these reports were not entirely true, and shows the misunderstanding of the metadata shared by NATO allies with the United Nations.
Other disclosures in documents leaked by Snowden suggests that the NSA has monitored the cellphones of 34 other top leaders of the world, including German Chancellor Angela Merkel.
National Intelligence Director James Clapper, while defendincting the espionage incident on allied countries, called it necessary and said that it was common for both sides to do so.
Amid the uproar, Murphy said his office was arranging a Congress yatra which is likely to take place this year, and expressed hope that the delegation would include members of the two parties and the two chambers.
The names of other legal manufacturers participating will be released in the coming days.
He said that work is still being done on the itinerary.
While Murphy said that the purpose of the yatra is to improve the relationship, he said some "impractional relationships" will also have to be sacrificed.
He said European leaders will also have to be honest with their people, their spying programs used for years.
"Whether we can revise our monitoring program to better protect the rights of the Europeans, but they also need to agree that we are not the only who are spying," Murphy said.
